# The device nodes /dev holds, one per line: type major minor name.
# type is c (character) or b (block); the majors are the driver slots in
# ciosw/biosw in sys/consts.c.  create_vol mknod's these onto every new
# volume, so this file is the source of /dev - not a blessed drive.
#
c 0 0 null
c 1 1 console
c 1 1 ttyA
c 1 2 ttyB
c 1 3 ttyC
c 1 16 cent0
c 2 0 mem
c 3 0 io
c 3 210 mediachg
c 5 0 net
b 1 0 hda
b 1 1 hdb
b 1 2 hdc
b 1 3 hdd
#
# The DJ-DMA floppy controller.  Two banks of four drives hang off it, the
# 8 inch port and the 5 1/4 inch one, and the low three bits of the minor
# name the drive: 0-3 the 8 inch, 4-7 the 5 1/4.  Bit 3 set is the same
# drive alternate-sectored - sio reads every other sector of a track -
# which is what these nodes carry; the unskewed minors are the boot
# diskette's own, mknod'd by create_bootdisk as /dev/root, not listed
# here.  fla-fld are the 8 inch drives, mfa-mfd the 5 1/4.
#
b 2 8 fla
b 2 9 flb
b 2 10 flc
b 2 11 fld
b 2 12 mfa
b 2 13 mfb
b 2 14 mfc
b 2 15 mfd
#
# The HD-DMA (MW) controller.  Four drives, named by drive and slice and
# not by model: a drive's geometry comes from its label, not from a table
# the minor used to index.  The 'c' slice nodes are below, with the other
# whole-disk slices.
#
b 3 0 hd0a
b 3 1 hd1a
b 3 2 hd2a
b 3 3 hd3a
b 4 0 ide0
b 4 1 ide1
#
# The NCR 5380 host adapter.  SCSI is a bus and not a drive, so these name
# targets and not units: the low three bits of the minor are the target's
# id, which is what the select mask is built from, and the type bits above
# them are zero because this card has one board and boards[] (sys/ncr.c)
# has one row.  Nothing here is a drive letter: a target reports its own
# geometry when asked, and what a slice means comes from its label like
# every other disk on this machine.
#
b 5 0 scsi0
b 5 1 scsi1
b 5 2 scsi2
b 5 3 scsi3
b 5 4 scsi4
b 5 5 scsi5
b 5 6 scsi6
b 5 7 scsi7
b 6 0 nd
#
# The CompuPro DISK 2 and DISK 3, four drives each.  Both take the drive
# from the low two bits of the minor, like every other disk here, and
# neither has a bank: the DISK 2 is the SA4000 controller, polled and
# driven through the selector channel, and the DISK 3 is the intelligent
# ST-506 board with an IOPB in memory.  A drive's geometry is its
# label's, not the minor's - same as the rest.
#
b 7 0 d2a
b 7 1 d2b
b 7 2 d2c
b 7 3 d2d
b 8 0 d3a
b 8 1 d3b
b 8 2 d3c
b 8 3 d3d
#
# The CompuPro DISK 1A.  One board carrying both floppy banks at one port
# block - the 8 inch drives and the 5 1/4 inch ones, switched by the drive
# select register's data rate bit - so the low three bits of the minor
# name all eight: 0-3 the 8 inch, 4-7 the 5 1/4.  That is the DJ-DMA's
# drive numbering; unlike the DJ, DISK 1 has no alternate-sectored mode,
# so bit 3 is not the skew bit here but part of the slice.
#
b 9 0 d1a
b 9 1 d1b
b 9 2 d1c
b 9 3 d1d
b 9 4 d1e
b 9 5 d1f
b 9 6 d1g
b 9 7 d1h
#
# The whole-disk slice, 'c': the drive at cylinder 0 with no roll, so its
# block 0 is physical block 0 and reading it is reading the label.  The
# minor is the slice in the high bits and the drive below - 64 is slice 2
# of drive 0.  This is the slice label(1) opens.
#
b 1 64 hdac
b 1 65 hdbc
b 1 66 hdcc
b 1 67 hddc
b 3 64 hd0c
b 3 65 hd1c
b 3 66 hd2c
b 3 67 hd3c
b 4 64 ide0c
b 4 65 ide1c
b 5 64 scsi0c
b 5 65 scsi1c
b 5 66 scsi2c
b 5 67 scsi3c
b 5 68 scsi4c
b 5 69 scsi5c
b 5 70 scsi6c
b 5 71 scsi7c
b 7 64 d2ac
b 7 65 d2bc
b 7 66 d2cc
b 7 67 d2dc
b 8 64 d3ac
b 8 65 d3bc
b 8 66 d3cc
b 8 67 d3dc
b 9 64 d1ac
b 9 65 d1bc
b 9 66 d1cc
b 9 67 d1dc
b 9 68 d1ec
b 9 69 d1fc
b 9 70 d1gc
b 9 71 d1hc
